he Landlord deal with the King. Let my teammate control the flow. I need to conserve my high cards for later. This King is likely a signal to force the Landlord to show his hand. I am supporting my teammate, not hindering him.  My hand isn't strong enough to lead.\n\nFinal Answer: *Pass*\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: My teammate has played a King, which is a strong card intended to force a high card (Ace, 2, or Joker) from the Landlord or win the right to lead. My hand consists of several isolated singles and is not structured well enough to "run" or finish quickly (11 cards remaining). Beating my teammate\'s King with an Ace would likely hinder my teammate\'s strategy and waste one of my few control cards without guaranteeing a sequence of wins.\n\nReason: It is standard strategy not to beat a teammate\'s high card (like a King) unless you can win immediately or have a very strong hand to take over control. By passing, I force the Landlord to spend a 2 or pass, allowing my teammate to potentially regain the lead.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": []}'}
